[THOUGHT] The caregiver did not provide the requested observational data — possibly due to inability to perform or interpret it reliably. Since objective physical exam findings are essential and cannot be deferred, I must shift to the next highest-yield, clinically accessible assessment: auscultation of breath sounds. Caregivers can often detect loud wheeze or stridor, but distinguishing true wheeze (lower airway) from stridor (upper airway) is challenging. A clinician-performed exam is ideal — so I will request formal targeted physical examination by the clinician, focusing on the most diagnostic elements for upper airway obstruction: inspection of oral cavity and larynx (for anatomy), neck palpation (for masses, bruits), and auscultation in multiple positions (supine, prone, flexed, extended). This is both feasible and necessary before imaging.

[TEST] Perform targeted physical examination:  
1. Inspect oral cavity for micrognathia, high-arched palate, cleft, or laryngeal web signs;  
2. Palpate neck for masses, thyromegaly, or vascular bruits;  
3. Auscultate anterior neck and chest in neutral, flexed, and extended neck positions — noting location, timing (inspiratory/expiratory/biphasic), and quality of stridor/wheeze.

